reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/CONCLUSIONS.md:19:Reconstruct identical saved candidate requests; interleave fresh no-score controls with true-score requests and a score-shuffled control, with fixed score representation and randomization, repeated draws, cache misses, and identical model, candidates and question content except score lines. Make each score's query origin explicit: multiple search queries may yield different scores. A shuffled control tests sensitivity to a number but needs care with singleton/equal-score cases and preserving query origin. A source-blind independent reader judges differing pairs and controls without seeing arm, score, or prior decision. Report wrong merges, missed repeats, incorrect revision classifications and group choices separately, all cases, with same-arm variability, cost and limitations; fixed-candidate comparisons do not prove forward-run performance. The fresh set has few or no revisions; use separately identified stored revision cases without presenting isolated pair results as product-path evidence. Freeze design, audit and preflight under repository rules before any paid send. A score can also encode context Jev did not directly receive (e.g. thesis wording or period), so improvement would not itself establish the mechanism.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/codex-review.md:13:   **Recommendation:** before sending, replay each selected article’s historical pre-article library and calculate each query’s complete ranked top ten, union order, all retrieval origins and every displayed score from exact cached vectors. Require equality with the saved candidate IDs and archived request. Freeze a per-case manifest and show a multi-query example, including a candidate retrieved by more than one query and a non-retrieving query.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/codex-review.md:23:5. **The new path’s decisive tests remain promises.** The design lists replay and refusal controls but gives no passing test evidence for score mapping, mutation detection, archive isolation or three genuinely fresh draws. [Design]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind/reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:32). This matters because `jev.ask` returns an archived answer for an identical request, and the archive keeps one row per request identity. [Jev archive lookup]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/jev.py:87), [archive behavior]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:74).
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/codex-review.md:25:   **Recommendation:** run a no-cost replay and negative controls that deliberately swap a score, query, candidate or origin and must fail; verify a known multi-query case and outcome tie handling; prove three separate archive misses with a fake transport; and test interruption, malformed answers and resume without silent replacement. Compare the complete serialized control request to the saved request. The existing Jev transport sorts the request before sending, so that comparison should use its actual serialization. [Transport]( /Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/jev.py:28).
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:7:C control: exact archived model/state/questions. T: each candidate instruction adds a newline and factual metadata: `Retrieval metadata (not part of the stored piece; cosine, not a probability): query 1 [thesis search]: 0.xxxx; query 2 [own-text search]: 0.xxxx`. Each query corresponds in order to the existing caller's query_vectors; its label and query type are made explicit without disclosing thesis text otherwise not sent. Include a score for each query, including non-retrieving queries; retrieval origins are recorded in the manifest, not sent. Jev returns no textual reasoning, so do not infer why its choice changes. Query type labels expose information C did not have, so any observed effect is the joint effect of score metadata and labels. No shuffled-score arm: it would describe intentionally incorrect scores as real, and its interpretation as a formatting control is uncertain. The test compares the practical intervention of adding real scores against the current request; it does not establish how Jev uses the numbers. Freeze this representation; do not infer importance from cosine or set thresholds. Decimal precision 4, cosine computed with existing compare.py and cached exact text vectors. Retrieval always uses original queries, no candidate changes. User requests testing this candidate, not deployment or any new rule.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:19:| Later run/config change/another writer/two scopes | Refuse existing output on rerun absent exact frozen manifest and explicit resume; do not access live data or concurrent Step 1 writes. Different scopes use different research directories and archives. | No runtime time used as identity |
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:54:D381 selects the candidate with highest Jev same-statement probability; this experiment retains it. D379 seals old product; no frozen files or production config touched. AGENTS §7.1 historical rulers unchanged; repeat reference is exploratory, not new frozen gold. AGENTS §7.2 read raw answer against source. Current Jev model `jev-1.14.0`. Offline census reconstructs and verifies all 185 comparison requests and answers. Standing preflight run against v108 archive judged 243 extraction answers and 170 matching records, two matching records skipped, 12 extraction records dropped and 2 stored schema refusals; that script does not test the clean Jev score path. The latter needs its own replay and refusal controls before send.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:58:Hypothesis: score information moves at least one relevant comparison and makes at least one source-backed decision better without a source-backed decision getting worse. Prediction: the clearly wrong X22 grouping becomes separate more often in T than C, while the selected accepted-repeat controls remain correctly grouped. We do not predict a perfect result. Predeclare individual decision comparisons, 3 samples per arm and show all 66 outcomes and errors. For each outcome and selected source-backed candidate, count correct and incorrect choices across three draws per arm. A repeatable improvement requires at least one agreed previously incorrect decision to become correct in at least 2 of 3 T draws and fewer than 2 of 3 C draws, with no agreed previously correct decision becoming wrong in at least 2 of 3 T draws while correct in at least 2 of 3 C draws. A repeatable regression stops the line. If neither occurs or independent readers disagree, report inconclusive. This is a screening rule for selected examples, not a quantitative estimate of product impact. Blind-read all eleven incoming records against all their candidate sets and source articles, including unchanged controls; report gaps. Register case ids and score mapping and historical top-ten replay before any send; send no paid calls until council review and preflight pass.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:64:Literal metadata is appended after a newline: `Retrieval metadata (not part of the stored piece; cosine, not a probability): query 1 [thesis search]: 0.1234; query 2 [own-text search]: 0.5678`. Four-decimal Python formatting. The label is `thesis search` for every thesis query, `own-text search` for the extra document query, never the thesis content. One score per query against each candidate group's original. Do not claim all queries retrieved that candidate. The manifest records all query identities, full top-ten lists, origin query indices per candidate, all vectors' computed scores and union order. Reconstruct the pre-article group order by replaying saved outcomes article by article, with no same-article visibility, and require union order and saved answer outcome to match all selected cases; fail on missing input. The manifest and source request/answer identities and case list are frozen with hashes before send.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md:66:Negative controls: mutate a query vector, candidate score, candidate id, and retrieval origin separately; each must trigger the independent check. A known multi-query case must have every query and origin checked, not only the union. A fake transport must pass 3 separate control draws with 3 calls, fail a malformed response and cover interruption; a missing input and empty population must fail. If an actual send ends ambiguously after dispatch, record it as unresolved and do not silently resend it; incomplete calls are not success. The only retries are the underlying transport's bounded policy, recorded separately where possible.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/codex-review-2-handoff.txt:1:We revised EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md in response: dropped shuffled arm, fixed 10-case 60-call small probe, specify all-query scores + complete pre-article top-ten reconstruction and manifest, blind source review of all ten full candidate sets, explicit 2-of-3 screening conditions, mandatory no-cost negative controls for mapping/archive/refusal. Independently re-read the revised document and review whether ready to implement isolated harness. Do not run paid calls or edit. Identify remaining blockers and whether rules can actually be checked. Challenge any overclaim; do not demand proving the result in advance. Return READY or NOT READY with reasons.
reports/step2-score-council-2026-11-08/harness-review-handoff.txt:1:Review revised EXPERIMENT-DESIGN.md and actual probe_prepare.py / probe_send.py before any paid send. User authorized isolated score experiment. Scope only Jev comparison, no production/Step1 changes. Check factual score/query mapping and historical pre-article replay, frozen request reconstruction, case/answer identity, metadata labels, archive isolation/real cache misses, fake controls, interruption and ambiguous calls, Jev API contracts, compare.outcome, safe handling and the need for source-blind review. Find any serious failure, including 11-case selection and C vs T semantics; quote code. Read-only, no edits, no paid calls. Return READY or NOT READY.

